course: dictionary
lesson: 3777
title: A baby's soft spot
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A fontanelle is a soft spot on a baby's head. Under the skin, the skull is not one solid piece yet. It is made of separate bones with soft gaps between them, so the head can change shape a little when a baby is born.
explain: The soft gaps close as the baby grows, usually by about 18 months to 2 years. Then the bones have joined together. This is why parents are told to be gentle with a baby's head.
explain: Context clues help you here: «A baby's skull has soft gaps called fontanelles that close in the first two years.» The words soft gaps and close tell you the meaning. A fontanelle is a soft gap that closes later on.
fact: A fontanelle is a soft spot on a baby's head.
fact: At a fontanelle, the bones of a baby's skull have not yet joined together.
fact: A fontanelle usually closes by about 18 months to 2 years.
fact: The American spelling is fontanel, with one L at the end.
fact: You may meet the phrases «the soft spot» and «the fontanelle closes» in science lessons about babies.
exercise: «A baby's skull has soft gaps called fontanelles.» What does fontanelle mean? | A hard bone in a grown-up's arm | *A soft spot on a baby's head | A small bone in the ear | A type of baby food
exercise: «The fontanelle closes in the first two years.» What does closes mean here? | Falls off | *Joins up and becomes solid | Opens wide | Gets bigger
exercise: Why are parents told to be gentle with a baby's head? | Babies like loud noises | *The bones have not yet fully joined, so the soft spot needs care | A baby's head is made of rubber | Babies cannot feel anything at all
test: When does a fontanelle usually close? | *By about 18 months to 2 years | Within a week of birth | At about age ten | It never closes
test: What is the American spelling of fontanelle? | *Fontanel | Fontanelles | Fontenelle | Fontanil
exam: What is a fontanelle, and why does it close? | A type of bandage used for cuts | *A soft spot on a baby's head that closes as the bones join together | A hard bone in the knee that never closes | A baby's first tooth that falls out
